# sports-athletics.kb - Sports / Athletics
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 5 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

hurdles | definition | Hurdles is running over a series of barriers spaced at intervals, and the distances are set so the race is evenly divided between speed and technique rather than being a sprint with obstacles in the way. The men's 110 metres and women's 100 metres use 1.067 metre barriers, while the 400 metres hurdles use 0.914, because the longer race allows a lower barrier. The 400 metres version is run almost entirely on the flat because there is no room to jump, which makes it a different event. | hurdles, barrier, distance, speed, technique, 400 | what is a hurdles race, how high are the hurdles, why is 400m hurdles flat | hurdles | hurdles
relay | definition | A relay passes a baton between runners, each running one leg, and the team result is the sum of the four times rather than the best individual time. That is why the fourth runner, the anchor, is usually the strongest, since a fast first three can absorb one poor leg. The rules are strict about where the exchange happens, inside a marked zone, and handing the baton rather than throwing it, because an invalid pass disqualifies the team. | relay, baton, leg, anchor, exchange, zone | what is a relay race, who is the anchor, what happens if you drop the baton | relay | relay
long jump | definition | The long jump is a horizontal jump measured from the take-off board to the nearest mark in the sand, and the technique is a run-up that has to be consistent and a take-off that has to be in front of the board rather than after it. The physics is that a longer take-off board distance gives a faster approach but reduces the margin for error, which is why the board position is the technical work. Most faults are called for touching the board or the sand, rather than for a short jump. | long jump, take-off, board, sand, run-up, fault | what is long jump, why is the take-off board so close, what counts as a foul | long jump | long jump
triple jump | definition | The triple jump is three linked jumps, hop, step and jump, and the discipline is that each phase has a purpose rather than being three equal bounds. The hop takes off and absorbs on the same leg, the step extends the stride, and the final jump converts the run into height and distance. It is the harder event of the two jumps, because the athlete must commit to a landing position in the air before knowing how far the previous phase carried them. | triple jump, hop, step, jump, stride, landing | what is a triple jump, what are the three phases, why is triple jump harder | triple jump | triple jump
sprint start | definition | A sprint start is the launch from the blocks, and it is worth more time than most athletes expect, since the difference between a good and a poor start is a fifth of a second. The three-point stance is standard: rear foot about two foot-lengths back and roughly a hand's width ahead of the front. The reaction time dominates the first phase, so a false start is penalised even when the athlete was ready, and the rules allow one false start before disqualification in most competitions. | sprint start, blocks, reaction, false start, phase, hand | what is a sprint start, why are starting blocks used, what is a false start | sprint start | sprint start
